4. NON-NEGOTIABLE DEVELOPMENT RULES

Follow these rules throughout the project.

4.1 Inspect before building

If a project or source code already exists:

- Inspect the actual files, dependencies, application structure, configuration, and current behavior.
- Identify which features already work and which are incomplete.
- Preserve useful existing work.
- Do not rebuild from scratch simply because a different architecture would be convenient.
- Do not replace the current framework or major dependencies without explaining the need and obtaining approval.

If this is a new, empty project, establish the architecture before creating the application.

4.2 Build real functionality

Every implemented feature must connect to its intended backend and perform its actual function.

Do not treat static arrays, mock responses, browser storage, hardcoded dashboard numbers, or decorative controls as completed business functionality.

Seed data may be used for clearly identified development demonstrations. It must never silently masquerade as live customer data.

Use browser storage only for appropriate preferences or temporary interface state, not as the authoritative database for customer, financial, or operational records.

4.3 Work in controlled stages

- Implement one coherent phase at a time.
- Inspect the relevant code before editing it.
- Make small, traceable changes.
- Run available type checks, linting, tests, and production builds.
- Test the actual workflows, not just whether pages render.
- Fix regressions before moving forward.
- Maintain a clear record of modified files and unresolved issues.

Never claim that a feature, integration, test, deployment, or security measure works unless it has actually been verified.

If a tool or environment prevents a test from running, report the limitation honestly.

4.4 Protect the project

- Preserve working functionality.
- Use Git and meaningful checkpoints.
- Never delete databases, migrations, customer data, or important files without explicit authorization.
- Never expose credentials, private keys, service-role keys, or secrets in frontend code.
- Never make production database changes without a reviewed migration and a recovery plan.
- Do not deploy publicly or incur paid services without my approval.

4.5 Keep explanations beginner-friendly

Explain what a technical decision means, why it matters, and whether it adds cost.

Do not overwhelm me with technical terminology. Keep the user-facing product professional and business-oriented rather than exposing its internal implementation.

