5. PRODUCT SCOPE

Build the product as a modular business management application.

A module is a group of related features that can be developed, tested, enabled, and improved independently.

5.1 Core platform

The foundational platform must eventually provide:

- Secure registration and login.
- Business workspace creation.
- Business profile and settings.
- Invitations and employee accounts.
- Role-based permissions.
- Subscription or license entitlement records.
- Notifications.
- Search, filtering, sorting, and pagination.
- File and document handling where needed.
- Audit history for important actions.
- Data export and account-management processes.
- Responsive desktop, tablet, and mobile layouts.

Implement only the features needed for the current release. Build the foundation so future modules do not require a complete redesign.

5.2 Customer management

Users should be able to:

- Create, view, edit, archive, and search customers.
- Store customer names, contact information, company details, and notes.
- Record customer interactions and follow-up dates.
- View relevant customer history.
- Identify duplicate records where practical.
- Export authorized customer records.

Use appropriate validation and permission checks.

5.3 Leads and sales pipeline

Users should be able to:

- Register potential customers.
- Track lead sources.
- Assign leads to employees.
- Move leads through configurable sales stages.
- Record estimated deal values and expected closing dates.
- Schedule follow-up activities.
- Record outcomes, including won and lost opportunities.
- View pipeline summaries based on actual records.

5.4 Tasks and work management

Users should be able to:

- Create tasks.
- Assign tasks to authorized team members.
- Set priorities, due dates, and statuses.
- Add descriptions and comments where appropriate.
- Track completed and overdue tasks.
- Filter tasks by employee, status, and date.

5.5 Orders and bookings

Provide a reusable, configurable workflow for tracking customer orders, service requests, or bookings.

Do not assume every business needs the same workflow.

The first release should support a basic record containing a customer, reference number, description, status, dates, assigned employee, and relevant notes.

Industry-specific booking calendars, availability systems, resource allocation, and complex scheduling should be separate future enhancements.

5.6 Invoices and payment tracking

The first release may include basic invoice records and payment tracking.

Users with appropriate permissions should be able to:

- Create invoices with line items.
- Calculate totals accurately.
- Track issue dates and due dates.
- Record payments and outstanding balances.
- Track invoice statuses.
- View transaction history.
- Export or print invoices.

Use decimal-safe monetary calculations and store currency explicitly.

Do not present this initial feature as a complete accounting system. Tax rules, statutory reporting, payroll, bank reconciliation, and country-specific accounting requirements require separate analysis and validation before implementation.

5.7 Dashboard and reports

The dashboard should display real information, such as:

- Total customers.
- New leads.
- Open deals.
- Outstanding invoices.
- Tasks due soon.
- Recent activity.
- Relevant sales or operational trends.

Every metric must be calculated from authorized backend data using a documented definition. Respect date ranges, permissions, and business workspace boundaries.

No fake numbers in production.

5.8 Settings and customization

Business owners or authorized administrators should be able to configure appropriate settings, such as:

- Business name and logo.
- Contact information.
- Time zone.
- Preferred language.
- Currency.
- Team members and roles.
- Sales stages.
- Relevant workflow statuses.

Do not allow customers to modify arbitrary application code or access another business's configuration.

