- Confirm users cannot access another company's records.

PHASE 4 — Sales and task management

Implement leads, sales stages, follow-ups, tasks, assignments, due dates, and status changes.

Verification:

- Test the full workflow from lead creation to outcome.
- Test task assignments and permissions.
- Confirm reporting reflects actual records.
- Test validation and error handling.

PHASE 5 — Orders, bookings, and basic financial records

Implement reusable order or booking records, invoices, line items, payment tracking, and outstanding balances.

Do not expand into a complete ERP accounting engine.

Verification:

- Test totals and monetary calculations.
- Test invoice and payment status changes.
- Verify authorized access and audit history.
- Test exports and printed documents.
- Confirm that financial changes cannot be made by unauthorized users.

PHASE 6 — Dashboard, reports, and settings

Implement real dashboard metrics, date filters, business settings, employee management, and basic reporting.

Verification:

- Reconcile displayed metrics with database records.
- Test date boundaries and time zones.
- Test role-based visibility.
- Test empty, loading, and error states.

PHASE 7 — Commercial licensing and customer onboarding

After the core product is stable, design:

- License types.
- Permitted user counts or other plan limits.
- License start and expiry dates where applicable.
- Customer onboarding.
- Setup and customization records.
- Support arrangements.
- Optional hosting and maintenance plans.
- A Diamond platform administration area.

Do not let a client-side flag determine whether a customer has paid. Any enforceable entitlement checks must be implemented securely on the backend.

Keep one-time licenses distinct from recurring hosting and support contracts.

Do not add automatic online payments until the applicable provider, fees, business eligibility, and integration requirements have been verified.

PHASE 8 — Pilot and launch preparation

Run a limited pilot with a small number of real businesses.

Test:

- Complete business workflows.
- Data isolation.
- Backup and restoration.
- Performance under realistic data volumes.
- Mobile usability.
- Error handling.
- Data export.
- Access revocation.
- Deployment and rollback procedures.

Prepare:

- Product website.
- Demonstration environment.
- Documentation.
- License terms.
- Privacy policy and other appropriate legal documents.
- Pricing packages.
- Support process.
- Backup and incident response plan.

Do not claim the product is production-ready while critical security or data-loss risks remain.

PHASE 9 — Future expansion

Only after the core product has paying users, evaluate:

- Arabic-first experience and complete Arabic localization.
- Advanced reporting.
- Industry-specific modules.
- Inventory and purchasing.
- Advanced accounting integrations.
- Employee and payroll functionality.
- Automated notifications.
- External integrations.
- AI-powered business assistance.
- Customer self-service portal.
- Self-hosted deployment.
- Additional payment providers and countries.

Prioritize based on customer demand, business value, cost, and risk.

