13. TESTING AND QUALITY REQUIREMENTS

Every phase must have explicit acceptance criteria.

Use the testing tools available in the project. Depending on the chosen stack, this may include:

- Type checking.
- Linting.
- Unit tests.
- Integration tests.
- Database policy tests.
- End-to-end tests.
- Production builds.
- Dependency audits.
- Manual browser testing.

Test successful operations and failure cases.

Examples:

- An invalid form is rejected with a useful message.
- An unauthorized employee cannot perform an owner-only action.
- A user cannot access another company's record by changing its ID.
- Failed network requests do not silently create fake success messages.
- A saved record remains available after a refresh.
- Deleting or archiving a record behaves according to the documented rules.
- A production build does not depend on development-only mock data.
- Secrets are absent from generated client bundles and committed files.

Maintain a list of failed tests and unresolved risks. Never hide a failed test simply to complete a phase.

